    {
      "title": "Knitting in the Round",
      "slug": "knitting-in-the-round",
      "displayOrder": 7,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 443,
        "lessonOrder": 7
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "With a ribber you can have up to 400 needles at your fingertips! <br>Did you know that you can knit in the round?  (no seams). BUT, remember automatic patterning (punchcard or electronics) only happens on the main bed.  So knitting in the round is strictly for stockinette only",
              "legacyComponentId": 5856,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "178356914",
              "title": "Circular (tubular) Knitting",
              "legacySlot": 1,
              "legacyComponentId": 5857,
              "order": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859457",
              "title": "Matching Ribber and Main Bed Tension",
              "legacySlot": 2,
              "legacyComponentId": 5857,
              "order": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<hr>\r\nAnother option is to create WIDE fabrics with both beds in work.  Instead of a tube, you can create a single piece of knitting that is up to 400 needles wide!  It takes a bit of concentration, and it will only produce stockinette.",
              "legacyComponentId": 5858,
              "order": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859462",
              "title": "Half Circular / \"U\" Knitting",
              "legacySlot": 1,
              "legacyComponentId": 6183,
              "order": 4
            }
          ],
          "title": "Put both beds to work",
          "slug": "put-both-beds-to-work",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3917,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Full Needle Rib",
      "slug": "full-needle-rib",
      "displayOrder": 8,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 444,
        "lessonOrder": 8
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "By putting all needles in work and knitting them at the same time you will produce a dense fabric that has knit stitches showing on both sides. True<a data-GlossaryId='184' class='glossaryhelp'> full-needle rib <i class='fa fa-question-circle' aria-hidden='true'></i></a>\r\nis completely reversible.<br>You will want to use a thinner yarn.<br>By adding stitch patterning on the main bed (color and/or texture) you can produce beautiful fabrics.<br>Adding multi-color knitting on the main bed while working full needle rib is called\r\n<a data-GlossaryId='268' class='glossaryhelp'> Double Bed Jacquard <i class='fa fa-question-circle' aria-hidden='true'></i></a>\r\nDouble Bed Jacquard. This technique is outside the scope of this course, but you MUST know how to set up and knit in full needle rib before attempting Jacquard.\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 5860,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859303",
              "title": "Full Needle Rib Cast On Techniques",
              "legacyComponentId": 5861,
              "order": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "The best bind off technique for full needle rib is a \"Kitchener\" bind off by hand. Yes it's tedious, but done correctly you won't be able to tell a tubular cast on from this tubular bind off method.<br>Don't be afraid of this technique, practice makes perfection!",
              "legacyComponentId": 5870,
              "order": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151858966",
              "title": "Double Bed Bind Off",
              "legacyComponentId": 5871,
              "order": 4
            }
          ],
          "title": "FNR",
          "slug": "fnr",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3918,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Practice Makes Progress",
      "slug": "practice-makes-progress",
      "displayOrder": 9,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 445,
        "lessonOrder": 9
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "One carriage setting and you can add texture and interest to your garments. <br> ",
